WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.940
A portfolio is most useful when you can talk about it.

2
00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:15.320
In an interview, be ready to explain why you chose a project, what problem you were trying to solve, what went wrong, how you validated your work, what you learned, and what you would do differently.

3
00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:22.380
The best conversations often come from tradeoffs and troubleshooting, not from pretending everything worked perfectly the first time.

4
00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:25.860
Use your portfolio as evidence, not as a script.

5
00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:34.840
If a reviewer asks about log analysis, point to a project that shows how you handled sample data, what signals you looked for, and how you documented the result.

6
00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:40.460
If they ask about application security, discuss a threat model or secure coding example.

7
00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:47.680
If they ask about teamwork or communication, discuss how you structured a case study for a technical and nontechnical audience.

8
00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:50.420
The project gives the conversation something concrete.

9
00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.100
Maintenance keeps a portfolio accurate.

10
00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:54.000
Links break.

11
00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:55.620
Tools change.

12
00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:57.400
Screenshots become outdated.

13
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:02.460
README files that once made sense may become confusing after your skills improve.

14
00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:05.040
Schedule time to review the portfolio.

15
00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:14.580
Update summaries, fix broken links, improve diagrams, remove stale claims, and make sure each project still represents the way you want to be seen.

16
00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:16.620
Pruning is part of maintenance.

17
00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:21.440
Archive weak, confusing, unsafe, or outdated work.

18
00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:25.540
You do not need to preserve every learning exercise on the front page.

19
00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:30.280
A project that helped you learn six months ago may not belong in your current portfolio.

20
00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:34.540
Keeping a smaller set of polished projects can make the overall portfolio stronger.

21
00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:36.100
Feedback helps.

22
00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:50.040
Ask a peer, mentor, instructor, or trusted professional to review whether the project goal is clear, whether the evidence supports the conclusion, whether any sensitive information remains, and whether the writing sounds professional.

23
00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:52.940
Do not treat feedback as criticism of your worth.

24
00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:56.000
Treat it as usability testing for your communication.

25
00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.360
Honesty is non-negotiable.

26
00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:00.380
Do not inflate claims.

27
00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:03.360
Do not imply production access you did not have.

28
00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:07.940
Do not describe a guided lab as if it were independent professional work.

29
00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:15.080
You can still present guided learning well by explaining what you changed, what you tested, what you documented, and what you learned.

30
00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:18.380
Credibility grows when your claims match your evidence.
